sysible-desktop-gnome (0.1.8) sysible; urgency=medium

  * state disable-user-extensions=false in the dconf system default. GNOME Shell
    sets this to true BY ITSELF when it believes an extension hung the shell, and
    when it does every extension stops loading — dock, desktop icons, the panel
    monitor — while gnome-extensions still lists them all and enabled-extensions
    still names them. Each one reports State: INITIALIZED instead of ACTIVE, and
    nothing anywhere tells the user. Seen in the field after a workstation took
    two months of Debian updates at once: the shell wedged, GNOME flipped the
    switch, and the desktop stayed stripped across every later login.
  * this is a DEFAULT, not a lock. GNOME flipping it is a real safety valve
    against a crash loop and must keep working; a user value still wins. What the
    default buys is that a fresh install, and every new user, starts with
    extensions on.

 -- Sysible <maintainers@sysible.com>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 12:40:00 +0000

sysible-desktop-gnome (0.1.7) sysible; urgency=medium

  * drop the dead [org.gnome.desktop.screensaver] block from the gschema
    override. GNOME Shell 43 builds the unlock dialog's background with a
    BackgroundManager given no settingsSchema, so it falls back to
    org.gnome.desktop.background — the screensaver schema's picture-uri has
    never been read. Setting it looked like configuring the lock screen and did
    nothing, which is the trap; the reason is now written where the keys were.

 -- Sysible <maintainers@sysible.com>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 13:05:00 +0000

